---
import Brand from "../../components/Brand.astro";
import Link from "../../components/Link.astro";
import Section from "../../components/Section.astro";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Trilo — multi-platform AI workspace | Alejandro Martínez"
  description="Sole architect behind Trilo: an AI workspace where autonomous coworker AIs participate as teammates. Live at trilo.chat across web, mobile, desktop and a Python voice agent."
>
  <div class="page"> 

    <!-- top bar -->
    <header class="topbar">
      <Brand />
      <nav class="topbar-nav">
        <Link href="/">← back</Link>
        <Link href="/#case-studies">case studies</Link>
        <Link href="mailto:alex@konar.es">contact</Link>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <!-- hero -->
    <div class="hero">
      <div class="hero-grid">
        <div class="hero-tag">CASE STUDY · 01</div>
        <div class="hero-main">
          <div class="hero-meta">MULTI-PLATFORM AI WORKSPACE &nbsp;·&nbsp; 2025 — NOW &nbsp;·&nbsp; SOLE ARCHITECT</div>
          <h1 class="hero-h1">An AI workspace with coworkers, not chatbots.</h1>
          <p class="hero-lead">
            Trilo is an AI workspace combining chat, projects, workflows, meetings and a knowledge
            graph, with autonomous AI coworkers (Luna, Atlas, Nova, Sage) that participate as actual
            teammates: persistent memory, tool access, a place in the team structure. Shipped from a
            single monorepo across web (Next.js), backend (Bun + Elysia), mobile (Expo React Native),
            desktop (Tauri 2) and a Python LiveKit voice agent. One architect, zero contractors.
            Live at trilo.chat.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-links">
          <div class="hero-links-label">LINKS</div>
          <Link href="https://trilo.chat" class="hero-link">trilo.chat ↗</Link>
          <Link href="https://app.trilo.chat" class="hero-link">app.trilo.chat ↗</Link>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- numbers strip -->
    <div class="numbers">
      <div class="numbers-cell">
        <div class="numbers-key">STATUS</div>
        <div class="numbers-val">live</div>
        <div class="numbers-sub">trilo.chat · paid plans</div>
      </div>
      <div class="numbers-cell">
        <div class="numbers-key">CLIENTS SHIPPED</div>
        <div class="numbers-val">web · mobile · desktop</div>
        <div class="numbers-sub">ios + android · tauri 2</div>
      </div>
      <div class="numbers-cell">
        <div class="numbers-key">AI COWORKERS</div>
        <div class="numbers-val">4</div>
        <div class="numbers-sub">luna · atlas · nova · sage</div>
      </div>
      <div class="numbers-cell">
        <div class="numbers-key">INTEGRATIONS</div>
        <div class="numbers-val">11</div>
        <div class="numbers-sub">gmail · github · linear · stripe …</div>
      </div>
      <div class="numbers-cell">
        <div class="numbers-key">WORKFLOWS</div>
        <div class="numbers-val">DAG</div>
        <div class="numbers-sub">custom orchestrator · checkpointing</div>
      </div>
      <div class="numbers-cell numbers-cell--last">
        <div class="numbers-key">MODEL ROUTING</div>
        <div class="numbers-val">2-tier</div>
        <div class="numbers-sub">fast-path / specialist · opik traced</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- the brief -->
    <div class="reveal-section">
    <section class="section" data-section-tag="THE BRIEF" title="Build the AI workspace nobody else is building.">
      <p class="body-para">
        One product that replaces chat, task management, docs, spreadsheets, video calls, calendar,
        and social media scheduling — connected by a knowledge graph that remembers context across
        all of them. The differentiator isn't the feature list; it's the AI layer. Luna, Atlas, Nova,
        and Sage aren't chatbots behind a slash command — they're coworkers with memory, tool access,
        and a place in the team structure. Trilo was designed from the first commit to be AI-native
        rather than AI-sprinkled.
      </p>
    </section>
    </div>

    <!-- architecture -->
    <div class="reveal-section">
    <section class="section" data-section-tag="ARCHITECTURE" title="Every surface, one backend, one monorepo.">
      <pre class="arch-diagram">  ┌─ trilo monorepo  (turborepo · bun workspaces · biome)
  │
  ├──▶ apps/web              [ next.js 16 · react 19 · tailwind · zustand · yjs ]
  │      └── lexical rich-text · real-time collab (CRDT) · stripe checkout
  │
  ├──▶ apps/backend          [ bun runtime · elysia · drizzle ORM ]
  │      ├── 325 service files
  │      ├── 18 background job processors
  │      ├── websockets + livekit integration
  │      ├── openrouter (multi-model via openai-compatible client)
  │      ├── opik LLM observability
  │      ├── composio toolkit  (gmail · github · linear · shopify · 11 total)
  │      ├── stripe (subscriptions · metered billing · connect/referrals)
  │      └── mcp/server.ts  ──▶  14 tool modules exposed to AI agents
  │
  ├──▶ apps/mobile           [ expo · react native · ios + android · apple IAP ]
  │
  ├──▶ apps/desktop          [ tauri 2 · rust · 33 source files ]
  │
  ├──▶ apps/landing-page     [ next.js · vercel ]
  │
  └──▶ agent/                [ python · livekit · real-time transcription ]
  │
  └─ packages/db             [ postgres via supabase · 55-table schema ]
                                          │
            ┌───────────────────────────┐ │ ┌──────────────────────────┐
            │   fly.io (singapore)      │◀┘ │   vercel (web + landing) │
            │   redis · docker · ecs    │   └──────────────────────────┘
            └───────────────────────────┘
</pre>
    </section>
    </div>

    <!-- decisions -->
    <div class="reveal-section">
    <section class="section" data-section-tag="DECISIONS" title="Six calls that shaped the architecture.">
      <div class="decisions-grid">
        <div class="decision reveal-item">
          <div class="decision-tag">01 &nbsp;·&nbsp; DECISION</div>
          <div class="decision-title">Bun + Elysia over Node/Express</div>
          <p class="decision-body">Chose Bun for native TypeScript execution and significantly lower cold-start overhead. Elysia provides end-to-end type safety from route definition to client — no schema drift, no hand-rolled validators. A bet on performance and developer experience over ecosystem maturity.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="decision reveal-item">
          <div class="decision-tag">02 &nbsp;·&nbsp; DECISION</div>
          <div class="decision-title">Custom DAG workflow orchestrator</div>
          <p class="decision-body">Off-the-shelf workflow tools assume human-defined linear sequences. Trilo's workflows are AI-generated multi-step plans with dependencies, parallel branches, checkpoint/resume, token budgets, and review gates. The DAG engine in <code>workflowOrchestrator.ts</code> was written from scratch because nothing in the ecosystem fit the shape.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="decision reveal-item">
          <div class="decision-tag">03 &nbsp;·&nbsp; DECISION</div>
          <div class="decision-title">Dual-tier model routing</div>
          <p class="decision-body">Simple queries hit a fast, cheap small model. Complex reasoning escalates to a specialist via <code>fastPathService.ts</code> → <code>lunaSpecialistService.ts</code>. Every path is traced with Opik for prompt observability. The result is cost-aware AI dispatch that doesn't sacrifice response quality for common cases.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="decision reveal-item">
          <div class="decision-tag">04 &nbsp;·&nbsp; DECISION</div>
          <div class="decision-title">Supabase + 55-table schema</div>
          <p class="decision-body">468 migration files represent sustained, iterative schema evolution — not a big-bang design. Drizzle ORM keeps migrations type-safe. The knowledge graph, workspace membership model, versioned pages, and spreadsheet engine all live in the same Postgres cluster, intentionally co-located for join efficiency.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="decision reveal-item">
          <div class="decision-tag">05 &nbsp;·&nbsp; DECISION</div>
          <div class="decision-title">Tauri 2 over Electron</div>
          <p class="decision-body">Electron was the obvious default. Tauri was the right call: a Rust-native shell with a smaller binary, no bundled Chromium, and a proper OS integration layer. The desktop app shares 100% of the React frontend — the Rust layer only handles OS APIs and window management.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="decision reveal-item">
          <div class="decision-tag">06 &nbsp;·&nbsp; DECISION</div>
          <div class="decision-title">MCP-first tool architecture (14 modules)</div>
          <p class="decision-body">Trilo ships its own MCP server exposing all core operations as tool calls — boards, tasks, messages, meetings, knowledge graph, spreadsheets, and more. Any MCP-aware agent (Claude, Cursor, Codex) can operate Trilo natively. It's as much a distribution move as an architecture one: make Trilo the workspace that AI agents already know how to use.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    </div>

    <!-- scope table -->
    <div class="reveal-section">
    <section class="section" data-section-tag="SCOPE" title="Every surface I built and shipped.">
      <div class="ownership-scroll"><div class="ownership">
        <div class="ownership-header">
          <span>PLATFORM</span>
          <span>STACK</span>
          <span>SCOPE</span>
          <span class="text-right">STATE</span>
        </div>
        <div class="ownership-row">
          <span class="ownership-repo">web</span>
          <span class="ownership-dim">next.js 16 · react 19 · tailwind · zustand · yjs · lexical</span>
          <span class="ownership-dim">sole architect</span>
          <span class="ownership-dim text-right">in production</span>
        </div>
        <div class="ownership-row">
          <span class="ownership-repo">backend</span>
          <span class="ownership-dim">bun · elysia · supabase · postgres · redis · drizzle</span>
          <span class="ownership-dim">sole architect</span>
          <span class="ownership-dim text-right">in production</span>
        </div>
        <div class="ownership-row">
          <span class="ownership-repo">mobile</span>
          <span class="ownership-dim">expo · react native · ios + android · apple IAP</span>
          <span class="ownership-dim">sole architect</span>
          <span class="ownership-dim text-right">shipped</span>
        </div>
        <div class="ownership-row">
          <span class="ownership-repo">desktop</span>
          <span class="ownership-dim">tauri 2 · rust · shared react frontend</span>
          <span class="ownership-dim">sole architect</span>
          <span class="ownership-dim text-right">shipped</span>
        </div>
        <div class="ownership-row">
          <span class="ownership-repo">agent</span>
          <span class="ownership-dim">python · livekit · real-time transcription pipeline</span>
          <span class="ownership-dim">sole architect</span>
          <span class="ownership-dim text-right">in production</span>
        </div>
      </div></div>
    </section>
    </div>

    <!-- outcome -->
    <div class="reveal-section">
    <section class="section" data-section-tag="OUTCOME" title="A live, multi-platform AI product shipped solo.">
      <p class="body-para">
        Trilo is live at trilo.chat with paid plans, on the App Store and Google Play, and as a Tauri
        desktop binary — all from a single monorepo, one architect. The technical decisions that made
        this possible: a custom DAG orchestrator instead of a workflow tool that assumed linear steps,
        dual-tier model routing that keeps costs manageable without sacrificing response quality, and
        an MCP server that means AI agents can operate Trilo without a custom integration. The deploy
        pipeline posts its own status updates into Trilo's chat. The workspace runs itself.
      </p>
    </section>
    </div>

    <!-- next case -->
    <div class="next-case">
      <span class="next-case-label">NEXT CASE STUDY</span>
      <Link href="/cases/satstream" class="next-case-link">
        02 &nbsp; Satstream — Bitcoin's data layer →
      </Link>
    </div>

    <!-- footer -->
    <footer class="footer">
      <span>alejandro martinez · @lyricalstring · oss under konar technologies</span>
      <span>alex@konar.es</span>
    </footer>

  </div>

</Base>


<style>
  @import "../../styles/case-study.css";

  /* ── SCOPE TABLE: trilo has 4 columns (1fr 2.5fr 1fr 1fr) ── */
  .ownership-header,
  .ownership-row {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 2.5fr 1fr 1fr;
  }
</style>
